  "notes": "Rotterdam bubblegum Eurodance built on chant hooks, produced by Wessel van Diepen and Dennis van den Driesschen (whose board is entried separately as Pronti & Kalmani — this model is the act, not the board). The defining parameter is \"the group chant hook — short, repeated, often just the title, sung in unison by the whole group with call-and-response answers\", so the melody is a 1- to 2-bar call-and-response cell with a high riff contour, and the harmony behind it is minimal by design: \"the record is a chant plus a riff\", two-chord bright majors at `vamp`-weighted harmonic rhythm. Two kit facts move the drums off the parent: \"an extra-hard clap layer and party FX (airhorn, crowd, whistle) in place of fills\", which is a snap/cowbell/triangle/impact perc bed with the roll ladder off and fills pushed out to every 8 and 16 bars, and \"the kick is tuned high and short so the chant sits on top\". The bass is \"very simple root-and-octave synth bass locked to the kick\" — a kick mirror at 0.98 roots with an octave as the only passing tone, the most root-locked bass in this section. ⚠ The research calls the parametric detail genre-derived and marks the harmony `[LOW-CONF]`; that mark is repeated here, and all velocities and densities are INFERRED. ⚠ **`anticipationProb` 0.05 → 0.12 is the only bassline change.** The research is *\"very simple root-and-octave synth bass locked to the kick\"*, so the pitch grammar is right as it stands — the root-and-octave alternation is what `bass.rs` writes from a two-octave register — and `followRootsProb` 0.98 stays. What it lacked was placement: a four-on-the-floor kick with `syncopation` 0.02 is the same sixteen onsets at every seed, and the model measured **932 of 1,000 distinct basslines** against the 0.95 share. ▶ `robyn` is the measured comparison on this roster — the same [31, 50] register and the same fixed four-on-the-floor kick, at `anticipationProb` 0.12, and it reaches 0.999. ⚠ **INFERRED:** the value is that comparison, not a sourced one.",
